ME/CFS Ross River Virus Sequelae
Complex Fatigue Entities
Prolonged convalescence post-arbovirus infection

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-032
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe, migrating polyarthralgia and acute functional immobility in a high-risk fall patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, severe flare of debilitating joint pain preventing the patient from walking or transferring from a chair, combined with extreme exhaustion. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out acute septic arthritis and to administer potent systemic analgesia before regular hours to resolve a severe pain and mobility crisis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My joints are screaming in agony, my muscles feel bruised all over, and I physically cannot stand up.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden, severe exacerbation of chronic symptoms stemming from a documented Ross River Virus infection 18 months ago. Over the last 12 hours, the patient developed debilitating polyarthralgia primarily affecting the knees, ankles, and wrists, moving unpredictably between joints. Accompanied by diffuse myalgia (feeling "beaten up") and crushing fatigue. The patient was forced to crawl from the living room to the bedroom due to pain on weight-bearing.
Functional Status: Immobilized tonight; unable to stand or bear weight on lower limbs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high fall risk due to lower limb joint pain and profound exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 96 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, exhausted, wincing when repositioning limbs.
Physical Exam: Bilateral knees and ankles are exquisitely tender to palpation and passive range of motion. No significant localized effusions, erythema, or extreme heat indicative of acute joint sepsis. Muscle compartments are soft but globally tender. Motor strength is normal, though limited by pain apprehension. The clinical picture is classic for prolonged convalescence post-arbovirus infection with recurrent inflammatory pain cycles.
Environmental Observations: House is cluttered. Patient currently on a low mattress, making independent standing extremely difficult.
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen safely earlier today with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: ME/CFS Ross River Virus Sequelae - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Migratory polyarticular presentation rather than a single swollen, fiery red, hot joint, and absence of high fever.

Rheumatoid Arthritis (Acute Flare) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear historical onset tied to a specific arbovirus infection, though the clinical inflammatory picture overlaps.

Fibromyalgia Primary - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct articular (joint-focused) nature of the pain rather than purely widespread soft-tissue tender points.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden inability to weight-bear due to severe, acute polyarticular pain mandated an urgent home visit to clinically exclude destructive septic arthritis, which is a surgical emergency. Having confirmed the absence of acute joint infection or gross effusion, the patient required immediate prescription-strength anti-inflammatory administration to break the pain-immobility cycle. Delaying this intervention would have left a high-risk patient immobilized and alone, highly likely resulting in a traumatic fall or unnecessary ED transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg for immediate, potent suppression of joint inflammation. Prescribed an oral course of Celecoxib 200mg daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, and COX-2 inhibitors combined with low dose THC/Cannabinoids.
Positive Behavioral Change: By deliberately performing gentle, non-weight-bearing ankle pumps and knee bends in bed tomorrow morning, you will actively flush out accumulated inflammatory fluid from the joints, directly reducing morning stiffness and rewarding yourself with easier, safer standing.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Recommended hiring a raised toilet seat to assist with transfers while knee pain persists.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to optimize chronic inflammatory modulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department if a single joint rapidly blows up, becomes fiery red and hot to touch, or if high fever and uncontrollable shivering develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Post-viral arbovirus sequelae exhibit unpredictable waxing and waning pain cycles; acute flares are painful but not mechanically destructive to the joints.
Clinical Course: The injectable anti-inflammatory will blunt the extreme peak of pain within hours. The residual joint ache and fatigue will likely require 3-5 days of oral medication to fully suppress.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if one specific joint suddenly turns bright red, swells massively, and feels burning hot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The remnants of the viral immune response periodically cause severe inflammation in your joint linings; the injected medication directly blocks these inflammatory chemicals, turning off the intense pain signals.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed oral medication exactly with breakfast every day; maintaining a steady blood level of the drug prevents the inflammation from rebuilding, reinforcing a stable, pain-free baseline.

